Sasha, thanks for describing loyalty (or lack of same) between past
and future selves so neatly.

The theory might also suggest that one's level of temporal self-
loyalty would tend to be stable.....if one feels betrayed by one's
past selves, one won't bother to take care of one's future self.

Here's another question about slavery--in libertarian terms, could
selling oneself be a permissible contract but not one that third
parties are under any obligation to enforce? 

Actually, now that I think about it, I'm not sure that there _is_
any libertarian consensus about how much obligation (if any) people
have to enforce libertarian rights. Any thoughts on the subject?

Anyway, assuming that there's some agreement in a libertarian
society that people have an obligation to return stolen property,
prevent assaults, etc., might there be an agreement that you
can have slaves if you can keep them yourself, but if they
get away, no one (except, perhaps, other slave holders) will
help you retrieve them?

Nancy Lebovitz


