>us lesser (im?)mortals, though: Fuller, Korzybski, Avogadro, and a
>host of lesser lights (how many can we think of?)
>
Barbara McClintock, who discovered a lot about corn genetics. 
I've just read her biography, _A Feeling for the Organism_, and
it describes her difficulties in explaining very complex insights....
Part of it is that she wasn't interested in teaching anyone who
wasn't as bright and interested in genetics as she was.

The book should be interesting to anyone who's into the history
of genetics, the workings of institutional science, the development
of new ideas, feminism, or the relationship between thought and
perception. This last is a major theme of the book--there's a
lot about how thinking more clearly about something enables one
to literally see more about it.

This relates to something I've been mulling lately--I don't think that
new ideas are developed in words--it can take living with them for
a bit (or longer) before they can be put into words. What are they
developed in?

I get them as feelings before I can put them into words a lot of
the time, but now that I think about it, the feelings relate to
something being there, and various ideas or descriptions fitting or 
not fitting with it. This suggests that the idea may not exactly be
the feeling, either.

Nancy Lebovitz


