Subject: [#94-9-605] Omega Point

 price@price.demon.co.uk  (Michael Clive Price) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-9-573:
>But who can feel threatened by a copy being tortured, when they, the
>original, are still alive?  And how does the resurrecter/torturer 

 Think of it in terms similar to MWI - the tortured simulation and
"original" have equal status as the future of the person who commits the
crime (assuming that the spacetime discontinuity in the worldline of the
simulation is unimportant).

>acquire all this data on someone on the run from "justice"?

 I can't find a convincing answer to this. The 2 possibilities I see:
 a) There is no need to guarantee that the torture will be possible; as
long as the criminal can't be confident of hiding the necessary info from
all future simulators, there will be some deterrence. I don't know how
hard it will be to reliably hide that info.
 b) Simulate all possible versions of the criminal and torture them. This
begs the problem of how to establish guilt (torture wouldn't be much of a
deterrent if it were indiscriminate).

 A few things Tipler said on Wednesday:
 - He clearly denied having a proof, although he did claim to be basing
his theory on conservative(!) physics. Looking back at the book, the only
claim of a proof that I find is on the jacket (apparently written by a
pr man). I think the proof that I imputed to him in my previous posts was
an argument from Hans Moravec about why the universe necessarily exists.
 - He said most well known physicists are skeptical to hostile (he mentioned
Ellis as particularly hostile), but he has gotten a better reaction from
younger physicists (David Deutsch thinks it's beautiful enough that it is
likely to become an accepted theory). I wonder if Kuhn's paradigm (which
Tipler claims has clearly failed empirical tests) is at work here.
 - He is indifferent to cryonics, saying it is a matter of personal taste
(it apparently doesn't suit his).
 - He hopes to have internet access in 6 months.
